why creatging a list of s3 buckets in terraform is bad, instead go with dictionary.

Using a list of S3 buckets in Terraform is not always wrong, but it becomes a bad design as soon as each bucket has its own configuration. A map (dictionary/object) is almost always the better choice because it provides stable resource identities, easier maintenance, and safer changes.

Problem with Lists
Example:

variable "buckets" {
  default = [
    "logs",
    "artifacts",
    "backups"
  ]
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "bucket" {
  count  = length(var.buckets)
  bucket = var.buckets[count.index]
}
Problem 1: Index-based addressing
Terraform tracks resources by index.

aws_s3_bucket.bucket[0]
aws_s3_bucket.bucket[1]
aws_s3_bucket.bucket[2]
Now suppose you insert a bucket at the beginning.

[
  "reports",
  "logs",
  "artifacts",
  "backups"
]
Terraform sees

bucket[0] -> reports (was logs)
bucket[1] -> logs (was artifacts)
bucket[2] -> artifacts (was backups)
bucket[3] -> backups
Result:

Resources appear to be renamed

Terraform may destroy and recreate resources

Dangerous for production

Problem 2: Cannot attach metadata cleanly
Soon you'll need

Versioning

Encryption

Lifecycle rules

Tags

Replication

KMS key

Logging

A list becomes ugly.

[
  {
    name = "logs"
    versioning = true
    kms = "alias/logs"
  },
  {
    name = "backup"
    versioning = false
    kms = "alias/backup"
  }
]
Now you're searching by index everywhere.

Problem 3: Hard to reference buckets
Imagine another module needs the logs bucket.

With list:

aws_s3_bucket.bucket[1]
What is index 1?

Nobody knows.

After six months:

Was logs bucket index 2?
Dictionary (Map) is Better
variable "buckets" {
  default = {
    logs = {
      versioning = true
    }

    artifacts = {
      versioning = false
    }

    backups = {
      versioning = true
    }
  }
}
Now use

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "bucket" {
  for_each = var.buckets

  bucket = each.key
}
Terraform state becomes

aws_s3_bucket.bucket["logs"]
aws_s3_bucket.bucket["artifacts"]
aws_s3_bucket.bucket["backups"]
These names never change.

Add a bucket
Simply add

reports = {
    versioning = true
}
Terraform creates only

aws_s3_bucket.bucket["reports"]
Nothing else changes.

Remove a bucket
Delete

artifacts
Terraform destroys only

aws_s3_bucket.bucket["artifacts"]
Everything else remains untouched.

Rename
Suppose

logs
becomes

application_logs
Terraform clearly shows

Destroy:
logs

Create:
application_logs
No accidental index shifting.

Easier configuration
variable "buckets" {
  type = map(object({
    versioning = bool
    kms_key    = string
    lifecycle  = number
    logging    = bool
    tags        = map(string)
  }))
}
Example

buckets = {
  logs = {
    versioning = true
    kms_key    = "alias/logs"
    lifecycle  = 365
    logging    = true
    tags = {
      Team = "Platform"
    }
  }

  backup = {
    versioning = true
    kms_key    = "alias/backup"
    lifecycle  = 730
    logging    = false
    tags = {
      Team = "Infra"
    }
  }
}
Very scalable.

Easier lookups
Instead of

aws_s3_bucket.bucket[2].arn
you write

aws_s3_bucket.bucket["logs"].arn
Much more readable.

Better with modules
Pass the whole map.

module "s3" {
  source  = "./modules/s3"
  buckets = var.buckets
}
Inside the module

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "this" {
  for_each = var.buckets

  bucket = each.key
}
No index calculations required.

Terraform best practice
As a general rule:

Use for_each with a map (dictionary) when resources have a natural, stable identifier (bucket name, VPC name, IAM role name, environment name, etc.).

Use count with a list only when resources are truly identical and order doesn't matter (e.g., create exactly 3 identical EC2 instances).

Comparison
List (count)    Dictionary (for_each)
Index-based (bucket[0])    Name-based (bucket["logs"])
Index shifts can force replacements    Stable identities
Hard to read    Self-documenting
Difficult to attach metadata    Naturally supports per-resource configuration
Fragile when inserting/removing items    Safe to add/remove individual resources
Poor scalability    Scales well for production infrastructure
Recommendation: For production Terraform, prefer for_each with a map/object (dictionary) for S3 buckets and most named infrastructure resources. It produces more stable plans, reduces the risk of accidental resource replacement, and makes configurations easier to understand and extend.


