Enterprise Architecture
Git

├── Service Source
│      ├── Image
│      └── Helm Chart
│
├── Configuration Repository
│      ├── Global
│      ├── Application
│      ├── Environment
│      └── Service
│
├── Secrets Repository
│      └── External Secrets Definitions
│
├── Release Manifest Repository
│      └── Application Composition
│
└── GitOps Repository
       └── Argo CD Applications
                ↓
            Kubernetes
The key principle is separation of concerns:

Service repository: owns application code, Dockerfile, Helm chart, and image version.

Configuration repository: owns ConfigMaps and non-sensitive runtime configuration.

Secrets platform: owns secret values and rotation, exposed through External Secrets.

Release manifest repository: defines which versions of services, configs, and secrets form a validated application release.

GitOps repository: owns deployment targets, promotion workflow, and reconciliation with clusters.

This separation allows independent versioning, deterministic rollbacks, auditable promotions, and controlled configuration changes across hundreds of microservices and multiple applications without coupling everything into a single Helm release.
